# 22. Full Secure Production SSH Setup

## Recommended Rules

```bash
sudo nft flush ruleset

sudo nft add table inet filter

sudo nft add chain inet filter input '{ type filter hook input priority 0 ; policy drop ; }'

sudo nft add rule inet filter input iif lo accept

sudo nft add rule inet filter input ct state established,related accept

sudo nft add rule inet filter input ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 tcp dport 22 ct state new limit rate 5/minute accept

sudo nft add rule inet filter input ip6 saddr 2001:db8::/64 tcp dport 22 ct state new limit rate 5/minute accept
```

---

# 23. Why This Production Setup is Secure

| Feature            | Security Benefit       |
| ------------------ | ---------------------- |
| policy drop        | Default deny           |
| localhost allowed  | Internal system works  |
| established rule   | Stable SSH sessions    |
| subnet restriction | Trusted users only     |
| rate limit         | Brute-force protection |
| IPv6 filtering     | Prevents IPv6 bypass   |

---

# 24. Dangerous SSH Mistakes

❌ Allowing SSH from entire internet
❌ No rate limiting
❌ Forgetting IPv6 rules
❌ Blocking SSH while connected remotely
❌ Using `policy accept`
❌ No `established,related` rule

---

# 25. Best Practice Recommendations

✔ Restrict SSH to trusted IP/subnet
✔ Use rate limiting
✔ Use `policy drop`
✔ Allow `established,related`
✔ Monitor SSH logs
✔ Use SSH keys instead of passwords
✔ Consider custom SSH port
✔ Disable root login in SSH config

---

# 26. SSH Hardening Outside nftables

Edit:

```text
/etc/ssh/sshd_config
```

Recommended:

```text
PermitRootLogin no
PasswordAuthentication no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
```

Restart SSH:

## RHEL

```bash
sudo systemctl restart sshd
```

## Ubuntu

```bash
sudo systemctl restart ssh
```

